WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.995
HTTP handlers often sit directly at the
trust boundary.

2
00:00:03.075 --> 00:00:05.728
They receive raw requests, decide which
method is allowed,

3
00:00:05.808 --> 00:00:09.258
parse headers and bodies, start or
continue context, call application code,

4
00:00:09.338 --> 00:00:10.400
and write the response.

5
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.558
A secure Go handler should be small,
predictable, and easy to review.

6
00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:16.929
It should not hide parsing, validation,
authorization, business logic,

7
00:00:17.009 --> 00:00:18.972
and response formatting in one large
block.

8
00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:21.547
A strong handler shape starts with clear
expectations.

9
00:00:21.627 --> 00:00:22.635
Check the HTTP method.

10
00:00:22.715 --> 00:00:23.830
Set request body limits.

11
00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.139
Enforce content type when the route
requires one.

12
00:00:26.219 --> 00:00:27.811
Decode only what the route expects.

13
00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:29.112
Validate the decoded data.

14
00:00:29.192 --> 00:00:31.049
Attach or read trusted identity context.

15
00:00:31.129 --> 00:00:32.138
Authorize the action.

16
00:00:32.218 --> 00:00:35.986
Then call business logic with validated
inputs and write a response that matches the

17
00:00:36.066 --> 00:00:36.703
API contract.

18
00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:39.543
Context propagation is especially
important in Go services.

19
00:00:39.623 --> 00:00:43.497
Request handlers often call databases,
queues, storage services, or other HTTP APIs.

20
00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:47.399
The request context should carry
cancellation and deadlines so downstream work does

21
00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:49.655
not continue long after the caller has
gone away.

22
00:00:49.735 --> 00:00:53.609
Timeouts and cancellation also make
services behave more predictably during partial

23
00:00:53.689 --> 00:00:54.963
failures or traffic spikes.

24
00:00:55.043 --> 00:00:57.007
Response behavior is part of secure
design.

25
00:00:57.087 --> 00:01:00.643
Stable status codes and consistent
response shapes help callers handle errors

26
00:01:00.723 --> 00:01:01.094
safely.

27
00:01:01.174 --> 00:01:04.836
User-facing responses should not expose
stack traces, internal configuration,

28
00:01:04.916 --> 00:01:07.517
raw queries, secrets, or sensitive
operational details.

29
00:01:07.597 --> 00:01:10.622
Secure handler design is not only about
rejecting unsafe requests.

30
00:01:10.702 --> 00:01:13.993
It is about creating a clean boundary
between untrusted input and trusted

31
00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:15.135
application behavior.

32
00:01:15.215 --> 00:01:18.187
Handlers are easier to secure when they
have a repeatable pattern.

33
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:20.973
Parse the request once, validate it once,
authorize it once,

34
00:01:21.053 --> 00:01:23.866
then call a service function with a small
set of trusted values.

35
00:01:23.946 --> 00:01:27.343
That structure reduces duplicated checks,
makes logs easier to interpret,

36
00:01:27.423 --> 00:01:30.820
and gives tests a stable place to verify
negative cases and edge conditions.
